@@ -0,0 +1,124 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `geohash` (georust/geohash, v0.13.2)
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest`
+v0.28.2 as a dev-dependency). All tests live alongside the existing tests in
+`tests/base.rs`, in a `properties` module (11 hegel tests: 9 green properties
+plus 2 deterministically pinned KNOWN FAILURE tests).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Generators: `geohashes(min_len)` — syntactically valid geohash strings over
+the real base-32 alphabet, length 1–12; `coords()` — coordinates in the
+documented domain (lon ∈ [-180, 180], lat ∈ [-90, 90]) with the exact domain
+boundaries and ±0.0 boosted via `one_of!`/`sampled_from`.
+
+1. **Encode→decode containment** (`prop_encode_decode_containment`) —
+   `decode(encode(c, len))` returns a cell (center ± lon/lat error) that
+   contains `c`. Tolerance: half-cell error + 1e-12 degrees absolute slack
+   (encode does one rounded multiply, `coord * ~1/180 + 1.5`; half an ulp at
+   p≈2.0 maps back to ~4e-14 degrees). Longitude compared modulo 360 since
+   x = 180 and x = -180 are the same meridian (the library wraps 180 → -180,
+   which is geometrically legitimate). Excludes the ~1e-13-degree sliver
+   below lat 90 pinned as a known bug (see below).
+2. **Decode→encode roundtrip** (`prop_decode_encode_roundtrip`) — for any
+   valid hash, `encode(decode(h).center, h.len()) == h` exactly (the decoded
+   center is half a cell from every boundary, so no tolerance is needed).
+3. **Prefix monotonicity** (`prop_encode_prefix_monotone`) —
+   `encode(c, k)` is a prefix of `encode(c, 12)` for k in 1..=11.
+4. **Bounding-box nesting** (`prop_decode_bbox_prefix_nesting`) —
+   `decode_bbox(prefix)` geometrically contains `decode_bbox(hash)`. Exact
+   comparison, no tolerance: the decode arithmetic is exact dyadic
+   (small-denominator binary fractions scaled by 180/360).
+5. **Precision strictly improves with length**
+   (`prop_error_shrinks_with_length`) — both error radii of a proper prefix
+   are strictly larger than those of the full hash (each character adds 5
+   bits, at least 2 per axis).
+6. **Neighbor is exactly one cell away** (`prop_neighbor_is_one_cell_away`)
+   — for all 8 directions, the neighbor's decoded center equals the original
+   center shifted by 2×error in that direction, with longitude wrapping mod
+   360 and latitude wrapping mod 180 (crossing a pole re-enters at the
+   opposite row — the library's observed N/S edge semantics, verified
+   self-consistent by probing before writing the property). Tolerance 1e-9
+   degrees, far below the smallest cell (~1.6e-7 degrees at length 12).
+7. **Neighbor opposite-direction roundtrip**
+   (`prop_neighbor_opposite_roundtrip`) — `neighbor(neighbor(h, d), opp(d))
+   == h` exactly, for all 8 directions, including across the antimeridian and
+   the pole rows (the wrapping is self-inverse; holds unguarded).
+8. **Validation/rejection** (`prop_decode_rejects_invalid_characters`) — a
+   valid hash decodes Ok; corrupting any one position with a character
+   outside the base-32 alphabet ('a', 'i', 'l', 'o', uppercase, punctuation)
+   yields `Err(InvalidHashCharacter(c))` naming exactly that character.
+9. **Decode robustness** (`prop_decode_nonempty_never_panics`) — `decode` on
+   arbitrary non-empty Unicode strings (including over-length ones) returns
+   Ok/Err and never panics. The empty string is excluded here because it is
+   pinned as a KNOWN FAILURE (below).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Two real bugs found; both are kept as deterministically failing pinned tests
+(per instructions, no fixes were made):
+
+1. **`encode` maps latitude 90 (and its next-lower double) to the south
+   pole row** (`prop_encode_lat90_containment`, KNOWN FAILURE). Latitude
+   90.0 is accepted as valid input, but the internal mapping computes
+   `y * (1/180) + 1.5`, which rounds to exactly 2.0 for y = 90.0 and for
+   `90.0_f64.next_down()` (89.99999999999999 — found only by the 10x
+   exploratory run). The bit pattern of 2.0 has a different exponent, so the
+   top-32-significand-bits extraction reads 0, i.e. latitude −90.
+   `encode((0.0, 90.0), 6) == "h00000"`, which decodes to a cell centered at
+   lat −89.997 — the north pole encodes as the south pole. (The analogous
+   lon = 180 → −180 wrap is the same meridian and therefore harmless; the
+   latitude wrap is a genuine containment violation.)
+2. **`decode("")` panics in debug builds** (`prop_decode_never_panics`,
+   KNOWN FAILURE). The empty string passes the length check (only
+   `len > 12` is rejected) and reaches
+   `hash << (64 - bits)` with `bits = 0` in `bbox_int_with_precision`
+   (src/core.rs:242) — "attempt to shift left with overflow". In release
+   builds the shift silently wraps instead. Expected behavior would be a
+   clean `Err`. The test's `one_of!` branch generating `""` directly makes
+   the failure deterministic; the shrunk counterexample is `s = ""`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact (zero-tolerance) longitude containment.** x = 180 encodes into the
+  cell starting at −180; since ±180 is the same meridian this is legitimate
+  wraparound, so the containment property compares longitude modulo 360
+  rather than being reported as a bug.
+- **Non-wrapping latitude adjacency for N/S neighbors.** The library wraps
+  latitude modulo 180 at the poles (N of a top-row cell is the corresponding
+  bottom-row cell). Rather than asserting "no neighbor exists at the pole"
+  (never promised), the adjacency property adopts the wrap semantics, which
+  probing showed to be self-consistent (opposite-direction roundtrip holds
+  everywhere).
+- `neighbors()` vs `neighbor()` agreement and `encode` vs `encode_iter`
+  agreement were considered and dropped as tautological — each is defined
+  directly in terms of the other.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Final run: `cargo test` — 13 passed, 2 failed. The only failures are the
+  two KNOWN FAILURE pins above; both fail deterministically on every run
+  (the lat-90 pin fails on every generated case; the empty-string pin
+  generates `""` via a dedicated `one_of!` branch).
+- Liveness verified: temporarily inverted the containment assertion; hegel
+  reported a shrunk counterexample (`COORD(0.0 0.0)`, len 1), then reverted.
+  No property hides its assertions behind a rarely-satisfied guard.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all properties, then reverted
+  to the default): found that the lat-90 bug also captures
+  `90.0_f64.next_down()`; the canonical pin and the sibling exclusion were
+  widened accordingly. No other new failures at 10x.
+- `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The workflow's "construct degenerate cases exactly" and boundary-boosting
+  advice paid off directly: uniform floats never hit lat = 90.0, and the
+  boosted `sampled_from` boundary branch is what makes the containment
+  property able to see the pole bug at all.
+- The "one exploratory high-count run" step found a strictly stronger form
+  of the pole bug (`next_down(90.0)` also affected) that the default count
+  missed — exactly the boundary-coincidence case the skill describes.
+- The Rust reference's guidance on `#[hegel::composite]` with parameters
+  (`geohashes(min_len)`) and on `one_of!` was sufficient; no API friction
+  encountered on this edition-2018 crate. `cargo add --dev hegeltest`
+  worked first try.
